nu overloading which  keeps the processing load  down while pushing its memory limits  extremely high this is proven by having  a pile of duplicated weapons on the  ground where by itself it doesn't seem  to affect the game too much  but as soon as you run an electric  current through it the game's frame rate  takes a heavy nose dive  trying to process electric effects  through 100 weapons it just touched  so the general idea of super menu  overload is to push beyond that limit in  a low memory area  using menu overloading to duplicate  weapons and overload the switch's memory  all while keeping the game from crashing  you're probably then thinking  how many times do you have to duplicate  weapons to get to this point and the  answer is give or take  600 times yep you heard me right this  takes anywhere from 25 to 45  minutes depending on how much trouble  the process gave you and on top of that  if you want to perform the technique  that was on the very beginning of the  video it's another five to ten minutes  on top of that  this is why even the most dedicated  glitch hunters would rather not even  attempt this because even with this  proven method being found  every time you do it isn't going to be  exactly the same that's right it's not  even consistent  and the unstable nature of this glitch  still makes the game crash a lot because  you're constantly teetering on the  limits of the game engine's capabilities  this means many attempts up to 45  minutes of duplicating weapons is wasted  as when you crash you start all over  again for this video i've duplicated  somewhere around 10  800 weapons for capturing examples  testing and retrying failed attempts  all to make this project happen  this is not for the faint of heart and  is arguably one of the most painstaking  glitches to perform in breath of the  wild  next to memory storage that said there's  one japanese user by the name of noho  who has performed this glitch hundreds  of times  and has recommended the 